WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:06.029
Do you ever worry that all of the wonderful
things that, uh, occupy the space in our lives

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:08.770
are built on, uh, human suffering and misery?

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.560
So a couple of weeks ago, Katherine and I
were on a little mini-vacation at a hot spring

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.980
in Jackson, Montana. A very small town, uh,
just the hot spring pretty much; that's all

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.160
that's there. We were on our way home and
we noticed a big sign for a thing and we were

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.609
like, "Well, we got a whole day to drive home,
let's look at the thing!"

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:28.349
My hair looks extremely good today. I want
my hair to look like this forever. I look

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.820
like Topher Brink from -- from The Dollhouse.
That's... What a horribly nerdy thing to want

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.050
to look like. Oh my goodness.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.730
Anyway, we saw the sign, and we wanted to
stop, and that is how I learned about the

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.780
Nez Perce War, the last of the Indian wars
in America. I've never had a particularly

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.600
good conception of what Native Americans were
like in eighteen hundreds in the west. There

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.640
are sort of two prevailing views: There's
the cowboys and Indians one where we're shooting

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.940
each other, and then there's the New Age-y,
in touch with the earth, seventh generation,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.150
use all of the buffalo, nomadic wanderer idea.
And there's probably elements of truth in

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.659
both of those, but what I would not have thought
is that Native Americans in the west in the

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:09.189
eighteen hundreds were both powerful and rich.
Now, I'm not talking about wisdom and spirituality;

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:13.250
I'm talking about militarily powerful and
economically rich. In fact, they were much

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:18.109
better fighters than the American cavalry,
and the numbers in these battles reflect that.

00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:21.140
There were far more American cavalry members
who died than Native Americans.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.259
What the Nez Perce Indians did economically
was really remarkable. In the fifty years

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.460
between when white settlers started showing
up and this last Indian war, the Nez Perce

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.170
were able to use the resources they had at
their disposal, their land and their knowledge

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:38.420
of their land, in combination with entirely
new technologies like horses and guns, and

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.249
carve out an extremely valuable and unique
niche in the American economy, which was making

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:47.040
really great horses for the American West.
That wasn't all that they did, but that was

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.979
sort of what they were famous for, and it
made a lot of Nez Perce very wealthy, and

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:53.420
it made a lot of Nez Perce tribes very powerful.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.579
So let's go real quick to the board. The board
is something that I've been experimenting

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:57.579
with. The board!

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:01.959
So I just wanted to give you an idea of what
caused the Nez Perce war. This is the original

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.819
homeland of the Nez Perce, and then this was
the original treaty that the United States

00:02:05.819 --> 00:02:10.989
government had with the Nez Perce. This was
their, uh, agreed upon homeland. However,

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:15.220
the fact that this area contained lots of
good cattle range and later they discovered,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.049
actually, lots of gold, became very problematic.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:22.370
So partially because the United States feared
letting the Nez Perce get their hands on this

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.390
gold and continuing to have this extremely
valuable resource in the form of land, the

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:31.680
United States compelled by force the Nez Perce
to sign another treaty that gave them this

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.040
land. Obviously, not a good deal, and many
of the Nez Perce tribes decided not to sign

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.040
that treaty.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.260
These are the tribes that we ended up going
to war with. This is where Katherine and I

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.360
were that day at the site of the Battle of
the Big Hole in the beautiful Big Hole Valley

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.909
where the Nez Perce were ambushed. They had
no idea that this force was after them. They

00:02:48.909 --> 00:02:52.129
were attacked during the night. The vast majority
of losses were women and children and the

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:56.439
elderly stuck in their teepees as General
Gibbon's force rode through their camp.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:00.819
To me, of course, hundreds of years later,
this does not look like a battlefield; it

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:04.909
just looks like a beautiful valley. We seem
kind of obsessed, uh, with remembering the

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:09.849
places where we lost battles. But I think
it's probably more important, actually, for

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:14.989
the future of our culture and even maybe our
species, that we remember the places where

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:18.160
we lost something more than that.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.370
Like, for example, our humanity and our ability
to tell when we're doing something wrong.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:27.829
In this valley a nation of strong and wise
people lost their lives, their families, their

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:32.500
possessions, their homes, everything that
they owned. They lost those things to greed,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.810
to complacency, to prejudice, and they lost
those things not because they were weak but

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.049
because they were strong.

00:03:38.049 --> 00:03:41.890
That's something that I think it worth remembering
every once in a while, John, I'll see you

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:42.170
on Friday.

